      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        The 0% Personal Income Tax Regime: Statutory Exemption &amp; Double Taxation Treaties
      </h2>
      <p>
        The most celebrated attribute of Croatia's nomad program is its codified fiscal immunity. Under <strong>Article 9, Paragraph 1, Item 26 of the Croatian Income Tax Act</strong> (<em>Zakon o porezu na dohodak</em>), receipts earned by natural persons based on remote work performed within the borders of Croatia for non-resident employers are categorized as non-taxable receipts.
      </p>
      <p>
        Key regulatory dimensions of this tax exemption include:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong>Zero Local Income Tax &amp; Surtax</strong>: Nomads are exempt from both federal personal income tax (15% to 35.4%) and municipal city surtaxes (<em>prirez</em>), regardless of whether they spend more than 183 days within the country.</li>
        <li><strong>Strict Domestic Prohibition</strong>: You cannot provide services, invoice, or deliver software consulting to any Croatian legal entity or Croatian sole proprietorship (<em>obrt</em>). Invoicing even a single Croatian customer immediately revokes your nomad status and triggers standard domestic income tax and social contribution liabilities.</li>
        <li><strong>Social Security (HZZO) Waiver</strong>: Unlike standard foreign workers, digital nomads are exempt from paying mandatory 16.5% health insurance contributions to the Croatian Health Insurance Fund (HZZO) and 20% pension contributions (MIO 1 and 2), provided they carry approved private international health insurance.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Application Procedure: Online Portal vs In-Country Police Station Filing
      </h2>
      <p>
        Applicants have two official filing routes governed by nationality and tourist status:
      </p>
      <ol class="space-y-4 text-slate-300 list-decimal pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Digital Submission (nomadi.mup.hr)</strong>:
          Recommended for all candidates regardless of current geographic location. Complete the electronic Form 1a, upload scanned PDF attachments, and select your intended primary police station (Zagreb, Split, Rijeka, or Zadar). You receive an automated administrative case identification number within 48 hours.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>In-Country Submission (Tourist Entry)</strong>:
          Citizens of visa-waiver countries (United States, United Kingdom, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, Japan, South Korea) can enter Croatia under standard 90-day Schengen tourist authorization. Within your first 90 days, visit the local <em>Policijska uprava</em> in person to lodge your dossier. Filing an application legally freezes your tourist clock while your case is under administrative review.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Certified Translation &amp; Apostille Protocol: Criminal Background &amp; Court Interpreters
      </h2>
      <p>
        Croatia enforces strict evidentiary authentication standards under the Hague Apostille Convention of 1961:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Police Clearance Criminal History Certificate</strong>: Must be issued by the federal authority of your country of citizenship or residence (e.g., US FBI Identity History Summary, UK ACRO Police Certificate, Canadian RCMP background check). State or local county police letters are regularly rejected. The certificate must be issued within the last six months and bear an official international Hague Apostille.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Croatian Court Interpreter (Sudski Tumač)</strong>: While bank statements and remote employment contracts in English are frequently accepted by MUP case officers in Zagreb and Split, criminal record certificates and foreign corporate incorporation charters must be officially translated into the Croatian language by a sworn court interpreter certified by a Croatian County Court.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Residential Lease Registration: Notarized Form 1a &amp; OIB Tax Number Issuance
      </h2>
      <p>
        Upon receiving conditional approval via email, you enter the final biometric onboarding phase:
      </p>
      <ol class="space-y-3 text-slate-300 list-decimal pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Lease Agreement (Ugovor o najmu)</strong>: Secure a long-term apartment lease. The contract must be signed by the landlord and tenant, and notarized before a Croatian public notary (<em>javni bilježnik</em>). Airbnb or hotel reservations are acceptable only for the first 30 days of temporary entry.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>OIB Issuance</strong>: Your local police station or tax administration office (<em>Porezna uprava</em>) assigns your 11-digit Personal Identification Number (<em>Osobni identifikacijski broj - OIB</em>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Biometric Residence Permit Card (Boravišna iskaznica)</strong>: Submit two biometric passport photographs, pay the statutory administrative issuance fee (~€42 via electronic post slip), and capture digital fingerprint biometrics. Your card is issued within 15 to 21 working days.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Production Python CLI: Croatian Digital Nomad Visa Eligibility &amp; Currency Validator
      </h2>
      <p>
        The following copyable, standalone Python 3 script validates bank statement CSV transaction exports against the official statutory Croatian threshold and calculates family dependent requirements:
      </p>

      <pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono text-cyan-300 overflow-x-auto"><code>#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Croatia Digital Nomad Visa Financial Requirement Validator (2026).
Audits bank account balances and monthly earnings against statutory MUP criteria.
"""

from dataclasses import dataclass
from typing import List, Dict

@dataclass
class NomadApplicant:
    full_name: str
    dependents: int
    stay_months: int
    monthly_net_earnings_eur: float
    liquid_bank_balance_eur: float

class CroatiaVisaAuditor:
    # 2026 Croatian Bureau of Statistics (DZS) statutory baseline
    BASE_NET_SALARY: float = 1015.724
    NOMAD_MULTIPLIER: float = 2.50
    DEPENDENT_INCREMENT: float = 0.25  # 10% of base salary

    @classmethod
    def calculate_statutory_thresholds(cls, dependents: int, stay_months: int) -> Dict[str, float]:
        monthly_base = cls.BASE_NET_SALARY * cls.NOMAD_MULTIPLIER
        monthly_dep_add = dependents * (cls.BASE_NET_SALARY * cls.DEPENDENT_INCREMENT)
        required_monthly = monthly_base + monthly_dep_add
        required_savings = required_monthly * stay_months
        buffer_savings = required_savings * 1.10  # 10% FX buffer

        return {
            "monthly_minimum_eur": round(required_monthly, 2),
            "lump_sum_minimum_eur": round(required_savings, 2),
            "recommended_buffer_eur": round(buffer_savings, 2)
        }

    @classmethod
    def audit_applicant(cls, app: NomadApplicant) -> Dict[str, any]:
        criteria = cls.calculate_statutory_thresholds(app.dependents, app.stay_months)
        
        salary_approved = app.monthly_net_earnings_eur &gt;= criteria["monthly_minimum_eur"]
        savings_approved = app.liquid_bank_balance_eur &gt;= criteria["lump_sum_minimum_eur"]
        
        overall_qualified = salary_approved or savings_approved
        qualification_route = "Monthly Income Route" if salary_approved else ("Lump Sum Savings Route" if savings_approved else "Failed")

        return {
            "applicant": app.full_name,
            "qualified": overall_qualified,
            "route": qualification_route,
            "thresholds": criteria,
            "salary_gap": round(max(0.0, criteria["monthly_minimum_eur"] - app.monthly_net_earnings_eur), 2),
            "savings_gap": round(max(0.0, criteria["lump_sum_minimum_eur"] - app.liquid_bank_balance_eur), 2)
        }

if __name__ == "__main__":
    candidates = [
        NomadApplicant("Sarah Miller", dependents=0, stay_months=12, monthly_net_earnings_eur=3100.0, liquid_bank_balance_eur=12000.0),
        NomadApplicant("David Vance (Family of 3)", dependents=2, stay_months=12, monthly_net_earnings_eur=2800.0, liquid_bank_balance_eur=42000.0),
    ]

    print(f"{'Applicant':&lt;26} | {'Qualified':&lt;10} | {'Route':&lt;24} | {'Req Monthly':&lt;12} | {'Req Savings':&lt;12}")
    print("-" * 95)
    for c in candidates:
        res = CroatiaVisaAuditor.audit_applicant(c)
        status = "PASSED" if res["qualified"] else "FAILED"
        print(f"{res['applicant']:&lt;26} | {status:&lt;10} | {res['route']:&lt;24} | €{res['thresholds']['monthly_minimum_eur']:&lt;10,.2f} | €{res['thresholds']['lump_sum_minimum_eur']:&lt;10,.2f}")
</code></pre>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Practical Renewal Roadmap: The 90-Day Exit Rule &amp; Transition to Permanent Residence
      </h2>
      <p>
        The most critical operational limitation of Croatia's nomad permit is statutory non-renewability:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-4 text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>The Mandatory 90-Day Exit Rule</strong>: Under Article 76a of the Foreigners Act, the Digital Nomad Residence Permit is granted for a maximum duration of one year and <strong>cannot be extended back-to-back while remaining inside Croatia</strong>. Nomads must physically exit Croatian territory for at least 90 calendar days following the expiration of their 12-month permit before the Ministry of the Interior will accept a subsequent digital nomad application.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The Permanent Residency Bar</strong>: Years spent residing in Croatia under the Digital Nomad Temporary Stay permit do not accrue toward the 5-year continuous residency requirement for Croatian Permanent Residency (<em>Stalni boravak</em>) or European Long-Term Resident status.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Transition Pathways to Long-Term Stay</strong>: Nomads seeking indefinite residency typically transition into a standard Croatian company formation permit (incorporating a local d.o.o. with €2,650 share capital and employing three Croatian citizens) or marry an EU/Croatian citizen to transition into family reunification residence.
        </li>
      </ul>

